        <body>
            
    <h1>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Learning Guide</h1>
    <p><i>Version 1.0 - Implementation Standards</i></p>
    
    <blockquote>
        RAG is a technique that grants Large Language Models (LLMs) access to external, 
        authoritative knowledge bases before generating a response, ensuring grounding 
        in specific, up-to-date data.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>1. Core Architecture</h2>
    <p>The RAG pipeline typically consists of three primary stages:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Ingestion:</strong> Processing raw data into manageable chunks and storing embeddings in a vector database.</li>
        <li><strong>Retrieval:</strong> Searching the database for relevant context based on user queries.</li>
        <li><strong>Generation:</strong> Passing the retrieved context along with the prompt to the LLM.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Comparison: RAG vs. Fine-Tuning</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th>Feature</th>
                <th>RAG</th>
                <th>Fine-Tuning</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Knowledge Updates</td>
                <td>Dynamic (Real-time)</td>
                <td>Static (Requires re-train)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Hallucination Risk</td>
                <td>Lower (Grounded)</td>
                <td>Higher</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Transparency</td>
                <td>High (Citations possible)</td>
                <td>Low (Black box)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Computational Cost</td>
                <td>Medium (Inference time)</td>
                <td>High (Training time)</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>3. Evaluation Metrics</h2>
    <p>To ensure high quality, developers track the <b>RAG Triad</b>:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Context Relevance:</strong> Is the retrieved context actually useful for the query?</li>
        <li><strong>Groundedness:</strong> Is the answer derived solely from the provided context?</li>
        <li><strong>Answer Relevance:</strong> Does the answer address the user's intent?</li>
    </ol>

        </body>
        </html>